I read the molecule as a macrocycle and wrote it out in four parts.

**Macrocycle backbone.** It runs in this order:
- a secondary amine NH on the left;
- the first ornithine α-carbon;
- C(=O)–NH;
- the second ornithine α-carbon;
- C(=O)–N(CH3);
- the tryptophan α-carbon;
- C(=O)–NH–CH2;
- a benzene ring;
- S;
- a pyridine ring;
- CH2, which closes back to the amine NH.

**Side chains.**
- Each ornithine carries a CH2CH2CH2–NH–Boc side chain.
- The tryptophan CH2 is attached to indol-3-yl, whose ring nitrogen carries a Boc group.

**Aromatic rings.**
- The pyridine is attached to the S at C2 and to the CH2–N at C3.
- On the benzene, the S-bearing carbon is next to the carbon bearing the CH2–NH group, and that carbon is next to the carbon bearing Br.

**Stereochemistry.** All three α-carbons have wedge bonds. Assigning CIP priorities (N > C=O > side-chain CH2 > H) with H pointing away gives S at each centre, consistent with L-amino acids. In the SMILES this corresponds to `@` for the first ornithine (written with its ring-closure N) and `@@` for the second ornithine and the tryptophan.

SMILES: CC(C)(C)OC(=O)NCCC[C@H]1C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCNC(=O)OC(C)(C)C)C(=O)N(C)[C@@H](Cc2cn(C(=O)OC(C)(C)C)c3ccccc23)C(=O)NCc4c(Br)cccc4Sc5ncccc5CN1

I read the molecule as a PROTAC-type conjugate in three parts, joined through a para-substituted benzamide:

- **Thalidomide-type end:** a 4-amino-isoindoline-1,3-dione carrying the 2,6-dioxopiperidin-3-yl group on the imide nitrogen. Its 4-NH leads into a chain of four CH2 groups ending in an amide NH.
- **Linker:** that amide NH is acylated by a para-disubstituted benzene ring, giving a benzamide. The other ring position carries an NH that forms the second amide.
- **Spiro core:** the second amide carbonyl sits on a pyrrolidine. Its ring carbons and nitrogen are:
  - C2, bearing the C(=O)NH group;
  - C3, bearing the 3-chloro-2-fluorophenyl group;
  - C4, spiro-fused to a 6-chloro-2-oxindole;
  - C5, spiro-fused to a cyclohexane;
  - the ring NH.

**Stereochemistry:** I assigned C2, C3 and the oxindole spiro carbon from the wedges and hashes, working out each chirality tag from the 2D coordinates. The cyclohexane spiro carbon is not a stereocentre, so it has no tag. The thalidomide-type piperidine CH is drawn plain and has no tag.

I checked ring sizes and substituent positions against the drawing: the oxindole is a six-membered benzo ring with Cl at C6, and the aryl ring has F ortho and Cl meta to the attachment point. The SMILES below is valid and matches the drawing atom for atom.

SMILES: O=C(Nc1ccc(cc1)C(=O)NCCCCNc1cccc2c1C(=O)N(C1CCC(=O)NC1=O)C2=O)[C@@H]1NC2(CCCCC2)[C@@]3(C(=O)Nc4cc(Cl)ccc34)[C@H]1c1cccc(Cl)c1F

I read the structure region by region and wrote it as drawn, with no corrections for likely drawing errors.

**Left end (pyridone):** The ring is N1H, C2, C3, C4, C5, C6, with a ring double bond C3=C4 and a ring double bond C5=C6. C2 carries an "O" label, but I could see no double bond to it, so I wrote C2 as an sp3 carbon bearing OH. C3 carries the CH2NH linker, and C4 and C6 each carry a methyl.

**Central six-membered ring:** It is drawn as a cyclohexadiene, not a benzene ring. Its atoms are:
- an sp3 CH bearing the C(O)NH group, where the "O" is again drawn without a double bond, so I wrote it as OH;
- an sp3 CH2;
- an aryl-bearing carbon double-bonded to a CH;
- a carbon bearing N(ethyl)(tetrahydropyran-4-yl), double-bonded to the methyl-bearing carbon.

**Linker:** The aryl group is a para-substituted phenyl carrying a CH2–piperazine. The other piperazine nitrogen is attached to CH2C(=O)NH–CH2CH2–O–CH2CH2CH2–NH, which leads into the VHL ligand.

**VHL ligand:** The tert-leucine carbon has a hashed bond, giving (S). The hydroxyproline ring is (2S,4R). Its C-terminal amide goes to a 4-(4-methylthiazol-5-yl)benzyl group. I derived the @/@@ marks by comparing against the standard VHL ligand SMILES.

